  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nClear skies and deep layer subsidence are expected to continue \ntonight as Great Lakes/Ohio Valley remain positioned between \nlongwave ridging across central CONUS. An expansive low level \nanticyclone from MO to the western PA will allow for winds to \nquickly decouple this evening. The extent of temperatures drops \ntonight is still a bit uncertain as low level thermal trough is \ndeparting to the east this evening and will continue to \nmoderate. This should be overcome by ideal radiational cooling \ncondition this evening. Some weak southwest flow on back side \nof the sfc high is possible across western Great Lakes later \ntonight, but this should not have a dramatic impact on \ntemperatures. Previous forecast still appears on track with best\nchance of mins at or just below freezing roughly along and east\nof Interstate 69, with generally mid 30s elsewhere. Frost \nadvisories and freeze warnings remain in effect for tonight into\nearly Sunday morning.\n\nThe primary storm track Sunday into Monday will remain across \ncentral Canada as broad longwave trough remains anchored across \nnorthern tier of CONUS. Some upstream height rises across Upper \nMidwest following departure of central Canadian short wave will \nallow for sfc anticyclone to be reinforced across \nsouthern/western Great Lakes early week. This could result in a \nlower end patchy frost potential Sunday night and Monday night \nas strong decoupling should compensate for slightly modifying \nlow level/near sfc thermal profiles. For the middle of the week,\na tendency for negative eastern Pacific upper height anomaly \nshould develop which will allow for downstream maintenance of \nmean upper level ridging and little change in position of low \nlevel anticyclone. A slow day to day temperature moderation is \nexpected in this pattern. The much drier airmass will maintain \nits grip across the region given stagnant longwave pattern much \nof this week, but given temperatures only slightly above normal\nand weak winds through the period, significant fire weather\nconcerns are not expected.\n\nSome lower forecast confidence exists for the Day 7 to 9 timeframe \nwith deterministic/ensemble guidance showing larger spread in \neventual eastward ejection of eastern Pacific negative height \nanomaly. Some indications another fropa could be possible late next \nweekend/early the following week, but this will depend on how the \neastern Pacific energy phases with broader northern stream trough. \nA dry forecast has been preserved through this forecast valid\nperiod.",
